Before engaging a processor, a controller must ensure the processor provides sufficient guarantees and that the processing is governed by a binding contract or other legal act with specific mandatory content.

1. **Sufficient guarantees**: The controller "shall use only processors providing sufficient guarantees to implement appropriate technical and organisational measures in such a manner that processing will meet the requirements of this Regulation and ensure the protection of the rights of the data subject." [gdpr#art_28(1)] Recital (81) elaborates that these guarantees relate "in particular in terms of expert knowledge, reliability and resources, to implement technical and organisational measures which will meet the requirements of this Regulation, including for the security of processing." [gdpr#rct_81]

2. **Binding contract or other legal act**: The processing "shall be governed by a contract or other legal act under Union or Member State law, that is binding on the processor with regard to the controller and that sets out the subject-matter and duration of the processing, the nature and purpose of the processing, the type of personal data and categories of data subjects and the obligations and rights of the controller." [gdpr#art_28(3)] Recital (81) similarly states the contract must set out "the subject-matter and duration of the processing, the nature and purposes of the processing, the type of personal data and categories of data subjects, taking into account the specific tasks and responsibilities of the processor in the context of the processing to be carried out and the risk to the rights and freedoms of the data subject." [gdpr#rct_81]

3. **Mandatory contractual stipulations**: The contract must stipulate, in particular, that the processor:
   - processes personal data only on documented instructions from the controller (including regarding transfers to third countries), unless required by law, in which case the processor informs the controller before processing unless prohibited [gdpr#art_28(3)(a)];
   - ensures authorised persons are bound by confidentiality [gdpr#art_28(3)(b)];
   - takes all measures required pursuant to Article 32 (security of processing) [gdpr#art_28(3)(c)];
   - respects conditions for engaging another (sub-)processor [gdpr#art_28(3)(d)];
   - assists the controller in responding to data subjects' rights requests [gdpr#art_28(3)(e)];
   - assists the controller in ensuring compliance with Articles 32 to 36 [gdpr#art_28(3)(f)];
   - at the controller's choice, deletes or returns all personal data after the end of services and deletes existing copies unless law requires storage [gdpr#art_28(3)(g)];
   - makes available all information necessary to demonstrate compliance and allows for and contributes to audits, including inspections [gdpr#art_28(3)(h)].

4. **Post-processing obligations**: "After the completion of the processing on behalf of the controller, the processor should, at the choice of the controller, return or delete the personal data, unless there is a requirement to store the personal data under Union or Member State law to which the processor is subject." [gdpr#rct_81]

5. **Optional demonstration of compliance**: "The adherence of the processor to an approved code of conduct or an approved certification mechanism may be used as an element to demonstrate compliance with the obligations of the controller." [gdpr#rct_81]

Under Article 27(1), the fundamental rights impact assessment (FRIA) obligation applies to deployers that are "bodies governed by public law, or are private entities providing public services," and also to deployers of high-risk AI systems referred to in points 5(b) and (c) of Annex III [ai-act#art_27(1)]. This applies prior to deploying a high-risk AI system referred to in Article 6(2), with an exception for high-risk AI systems intended to be used in the area listed in point 2 of Annex III [ai-act#art_27(1)]. Recital (96) similarly states that "deployers of high-risk AI systems that are bodies governed by public law, or private entities providing public services and deployers of certain high-risk AI systems listed in an annex to this Regulation, such as banking or insurance entities, should carry out a fundamental rights impact assessment prior to putting it into use" [ai-act#rct_96].

The assessment must cover the following elements, per Article 27(1)(a)–(f) [ai-act#art_27(1)]:
- (a) "a description of the deployer’s processes in which the high-risk AI system will be used in line with its intended purpose";
- (b) "a description of the period of time within which, and the frequency with which, each high-risk AI system is intended to be used";
- (c) "the categories of natural persons and groups likely to be affected by its use in the specific context";
- (d) "the specific risks of harm likely to have an impact on the categories of natural persons or groups of persons identified pursuant to point (c) of this paragraph, taking into account the information given by the provider pursuant to Article 13";
- (e) "a description of the implementation of human oversight measures, according to the instructions for use";
- (f) "the measures to be taken in the case of the materialisation of those risks, including the arrangements for internal governance and complaint mechanisms."

The obligation applies to the first use of the high-risk AI system, and the deployer may rely on previously conducted FRIAs or existing impact assessments carried out by the provider in similar cases; if any element changes or is no longer up to date, the deployer must update the information [ai-act#art_27(2)]. If obligations are already met through a data protection impact assessment under Article 35 of Regulation (EU) 2016/679 or Article 27 of Directive (EU) 2016/680, the deployer may include cross-references to or relevant parts of that assessment in the FRIA [ai-act#art_27(4)]. The AI Office must develop a template for a questionnaire, including through an automated tool, to facilitate compliance, and this template may allow cross-references to the data protection impact assessment [ai-act#art_27(5)].
